  "title": "English verb tenses: the big picture",
  "lesson": {
    "kind": "intro",
    "focusPoint": null,
    "points": [
      {
        "id": "p1",
        "heading": "Point 1: Review of the last class",
        "body": "What did you learn in the last class?\n\nLast week you learned that a verb is an action word, like sing, run or eat. Today we ride the Mérida cable car."
      },
      {
        "id": "p2",
        "heading": "Point 2: A verb tells us when",
        "body": "The Mérida cable car climbs in sections. How does a verb tell us when it happens?\n\nEach station is a different time: before, now and later. The verb changes to show it. These are tenses (tiempos)."
      },
      {
        "id": "p3",
        "heading": "Point 3: One-word tenses",
        "body": "You stand at a station and you sing. What do you say now? And yesterday?\n\nNow you say “I sing”: simple present. Before, you say “I sang”: simple past. Both tenses use only one word."
      },
      {
        "id": "p4",
        "heading": "Point 4: Simple future",
        "body": "Tomorrow you ride the cable car again. How do you say that you will sing?\n\nYou add the helper word will: “I will sing” is the simple future. The verb after will does not change."
      },
      {
        "id": "p5",
        "heading": "Point 5: Helper words",
        "body": "Some trips need two sections. Can a verb use two or three words to tell when?\n\n“I was singing”: past continuous. “I have sung”: present perfect. Was and have are helper verbs."
      },
      {
        "id": "p6",
        "heading": "Point 6: One word or more words",
        "body": "Look at the five tenses together. Which use one word, which use more?\n\nOne word: I sing, I sang. More words: I will sing, I was singing, I have sung."
      }
    ],
    "summary": "A verb tells us when: I sing, I sang, I will sing, I was singing, I have sung.",
